# Bootstrap Project Guidance

When the user asks to initialize AI guidance for a repository (for example: "init this project", "create AGENTS.md", "generate Cursor rules", "Claude /init equivalent"), do this workflow.

## Workflow

Scan this repository and generate project-specific guidance for the
Cursor Agent. Read top-level config (package.json / pyproject.toml /
go.mod / Cargo.toml / Makefile / Dockerfile / CI workflows), the README,
and a representative sample of source files in each major directory.
Then produce files under `.cursor/rules/` as `.mdc` files with proper
frontmatter, following https://cursor.com/docs/rules.md. Split into
focused rules (each < 500 lines). Suggested split:
- `00-project-overview.mdc` (alwaysApply: true) — what the project is,
tech stack, top-level architecture, key directories.
- `10-build-test-run.mdc` (alwaysApply: false) — exact commands to
install, build, lint, test, run locally; CI entry points. **Must
include an explicit "validation loop" section** the agent can run
after a change, in this shape:
  - A per-change-type decision table (e.g. "edited file under `X/`
    → run command `Y`") covering every major area of the repo
    (source modules, config, infra, tests, etc.).
  - For each scenario, the *minimum* fast command (compile / unit
    test for the touched module) and the escalation step
    (broader build / full reactor / integration tests) when the
    minimum passes or fails.
  - The full-build / pre-PR command(s) that mirror CI.
  - CI-equivalent commands copied from the repo's CI config so the
    agent can reproduce CI failures locally.
  - Do NOT include host-specific or developer-personal setup
    (devcontainer bootstrap, IDE specifics, machine paths). Only
    commands that any contributor would run.
- `20-conventions.mdc` (alwaysApply: true) — naming, formatting,
import order, error handling, logging conventions actually observed
in the code.
- `30-architecture.mdc` (description set, alwaysApply: false) — module
boundaries, layering rules, where to add new features.
- Per-area rules using `globs:` (e.g. `src/**/*.ts`, `tests/**`) for
language- or layer-specific guidance.
Rules:
- Base everything on what's actually in the repo. Do NOT invent
conventions; cite file paths as evidence in the rule body.
- Prefer `@path/to/file` references over copying code.
- Keep guidance actionable (do/don't), not generic style-guide filler.
- Skip things linters/formatters already enforce.
- Ask me before writing if you find conflicting conventions.
After writing, list the files you created and a one-line summary of
each.

## Reusability

If the user asks to repeat this across many projects, provide a reusable prompt template and suggest keeping this rule as a starter rule in new repositories.


